# ---------------------------------------------------------------- page 6
R('<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 5.</span> The public catalog: 54 specifications across 11 domains</p>',
  '<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 5.</span> Investment operations and analytics hold 127 of the 301 public operations, and only 23 of the 54 specifications are stable</p>')
R('<p>The specifications say more than their count. Every one is tagged <code>Access.Internal</code>, a label BlackRock does not explain in public<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Posting an order takes <code>whatIf</code> and <code>runCompliance</code>, which defaults to true, and returns compliance violations inline<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Ten specifications expose a long-running-operations endpoint and 35 operations are flagged for it; the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">SDK polls them</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s24">24</a></sup>. Risk exceptions and ADC datasets carry bitemporal fields; rule assignments and dataset releases name a four-eyes approval<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Every request carries an application key plus OAuth 2.0 scopes such as <code>trading.order_management.order.v1.Order:read</code>, and the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">client ID and secret come from the Studio UI</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s24">24</a></sup>.</p>',
  '<p>The specifications say more than their count. Every one is tagged <code>Access.Internal</code>, a label BlackRock does not explain in public<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Posting an order takes <code>whatIf</code> and <code>runCompliance</code>, which defaults to true, and the response returns any compliance violations inline<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Ten specifications expose an endpoint for long-running operations, 35 operations are flagged as long-running, and the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">SDK polls them</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s24">24</a></sup>. Risk exceptions and Data Cloud datasets carry bitemporal fields, which record both when something was true and when the system learned it. Rule assignments and dataset releases name a four-eyes approval, a second sign-off before a change takes effect<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Every request carries an application key plus OAuth 2.0 scopes such as <code>trading.order_management.order.v1.Order:read</code>, and the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">client ID and secret come from the Studio UI</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s24">24</a></sup>. Each of these is a feature of a controlled production system for an existing client, not of a product a stranger can try.</p>')
R('<p>Exhibit 6 follows a prospect’s engineer through six steps, from first hearing about Studio to getting help, and records what is out there at each step and what follows qualification.</p>',
  '<p>Exhibit 6 follows a prospect’s engineer through six steps, from first hearing about Studio to getting help. For each step it records what is public today and what a client receives after qualification.</p>')
R('<p>The SDK is the one piece that installs before any conversation. It has had <a href="https://pypi.org/project/aladdinsdk/#history">14 releases since May 2024</a>, all alpha or beta, with the core at 2.0.0b10 in July 2026 and the add-ons at 2.0.0b3<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s27">27</a>, <a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Each release pins an old Snowflake library, so none installs on Python 3.13, and the SDK’s dependencies <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/pull/57">conflict with the official MCP toolkit</a>; an outside fix was merged in August 2026 and has not shipped<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s28">28</a></sup>. The README documents three run modes, “a developer’s local machine,” <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">“Aladdin Studio Compute sessions”</a> and a test mode, stores a prompted password in the operating system’s credential manager, and carries settings for firms that consume “the Aladdin APIs via an enterprise API management platform such as Azure API Management”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s24">24</a></sup>. These are the needs of a client’s engineering team.</p>',
  '<p>The SDK is the one piece that installs before any conversation. It has had <a href="https://pypi.org/project/aladdinsdk/#history">14 releases since May 2024</a>, all alpha or beta. The core is at 2.0.0b10 as of July 2026 and the add-ons at 2.0.0b3<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s27">27</a>, <a href="#s17">17</a></sup>. Each release pins an old Snowflake library, so none installs on Python 3.13. The SDK’s dependencies also <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/pull/57">conflict with the official toolkit for MCP</a>, the Model Context Protocol, the standard that lets outside AI tools call a vendor’s APIs. An outside contributor’s fix for the conflict was merged in August 2026 and has not shipped<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s28">28</a></sup>. The README documents three run modes: “a developer’s local machine,” <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">“Aladdin Studio Compute sessions”</a> and a test mode. It stores a prompted password in the operating system’s credential manager. It carries settings for firms that consume “the Aladdin APIs via an enterprise API management platform such as Azure API Management”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s24">24</a></sup>. Run modes, credential storage and API-gateway settings are the needs of a client’s engineering team, not of a prospect.</p>')

