R('<h2>1. Public customer proof already exists, and it is viable even though it is not Studio-specific</h2>',
  '<h2>1. Public proof that clients use Aladdin already exists, and it works as Studio proof even though none of it names Studio</h2>')
R('finds no filing mentioning it<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s43">43</a></sup>. Proof of Aladdin in use is public all the same. On BlackRock’s client video, Royal London’s chief executive credits <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/client-story/client-testimonial-royal-london-asset-management">the Snowflake implementation</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s44">44</a></sup>. JPMorgan Chase’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&amp;CIK=0000019617&amp;type=DEF+14A">proxy statement</a> reports Aladdin payments of $4.4 million to $7.6 million a year from 2017 to 2023, then $14.7 million in 2024 and <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/19617/000001961726000096/jpm-20260402.htm">$25.6 million in 2025</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s2">2</a>, <a href="#s3">3</a></sup>.</p>',
  'finds no filing that mentions Studio<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s43">43</a></sup>. Proof that clients use Aladdin, though, is public. On BlackRock’s client video, Royal London’s chief executive credits <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/client-story/client-testimonial-royal-london-asset-management">the Snowflake implementation</a> done as part of its Aladdin programme<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s44">44</a></sup>. JPMorgan Chase’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&amp;CIK=0000019617&amp;type=DEF+14A">proxy statements</a> report payments for Aladdin of $4.4 million to $7.6 million a year from 2017 to 2023. The figure then jumped to $14.7 million in 2024 and <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/19617/000001961726000096/jpm-20260402.htm">$25.6 million in 2025</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s2">2</a>, <a href="#s3">3</a></sup>.</p>')
R('<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 10.</span> JPMorgan Chase payments to BlackRock for Aladdin, $ millions</p>',
  '<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 10.</span> JPMorgan Chase paid BlackRock under $8 million a year for Aladdin from 2017 to 2023, then $14.7 million in 2024 and $25.6 million in 2025</p>')
R('Source: JPMorgan Chase proxy statements (DEF 14A), filed 2017 to 2026, by year of payment<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s2">2</a>, <a href="#s3">3</a></sup>. The payments may include more than a client license: JPMorgan is also a custodian, and <a href="https://www.thetradenews.com/jp-morgan-to-connect-to-blackrocks-aladdin/">in 2016 it said it was connecting to Aladdin</a> to serve custody clients on the platform<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s45">45</a></sup>; that report is trade press, not a JPMorgan or BlackRock document.</p>',
  'Source: JPMorgan Chase proxy statements (DEF 14A), filed 2017 to 2026, by year of payment<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s2">2</a>, <a href="#s3">3</a></sup>. The grey 2016 bar is a payment for integration services, not a payment for Aladdin, and is excluded from the figures in the text. The payments may cover more than a client license. JPMorgan is also a custodian, and <a href="https://www.thetradenews.com/jp-morgan-to-connect-to-blackrocks-aladdin/">in 2016 it said it was connecting to Aladdin</a> to serve custody clients on the platform<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s45">45</a></sup>. That 2016 report is trade press, not a JPMorgan or BlackRock document.</p>')
R('<p>In December 2025 a Janus Henderson engineer submitted two changes to the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">open-source SDK</a>, <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/pull/52">URL rewriting</a> and <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/pull/53">custom headers</a>, for firms that route Aladdin calls through an enterprise gateway; the BlackRock engineer who co-wrote the SDK’s <a href="https://engineering.blackrock.com/open-sourcing-the-aladdinsdk-empower-python-developers-with-a-quantitative-edge-7f63376061e6">launch post</a> merged them, and the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/commit/1c1bd2559e4e4da2591f13dccfd7db6c2f9dc68c.patch">commits</a> carry a janushenderson.com author line<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s31">31</a>, <a href="#s46">46</a>, <a href="#s47">47</a>, <a href="#s48">48</a></sup>. Janus Henderson’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1274173/000143774926005628/jhg20251231_10k.htm">10-K</a> says the firm chose in the third quarter of 2025 to move its investment management system to Aladdin, “a complex, multi-year initiative”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s49">49</a></sup>. Public buyers add a third record: procurement notices that score the bids.</p>',
  '<p>In December 2025 a Janus Henderson engineer submitted two changes to the <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk">open-source SDK</a>: <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/pull/52">URL rewriting</a> and <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/pull/53">custom headers</a>, both for firms that route Aladdin calls through an enterprise gateway. The BlackRock engineer who co-wrote the SDK’s <a href="https://engineering.blackrock.com/open-sourcing-the-aladdinsdk-empower-python-developers-with-a-quantitative-edge-7f63376061e6">launch post</a> merged them. The <a href="https://github.com/blackrock/aladdinsdk/commit/1c1bd2559e4e4da2591f13dccfd7db6c2f9dc68c.patch">commits</a> name a janushenderson.com address as the author<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s31">31</a>, <a href="#s46">46</a>, <a href="#s47">47</a>, <a href="#s48">48</a></sup>. Janus Henderson’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1274173/000143774926005628/jhg20251231_10k.htm">10-K</a> says that in the third quarter of 2025 the firm chose to move its investment management system to Aladdin, “a complex, multi-year initiative”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s49">49</a></sup>. Public buyers add a third kind of record. Their procurement notices show how each bid for Aladdin was scored.</p>')
