# ---------------------------------------------------------------- page 10, Finding 2
R('<h2>2. BlackRock’s growth story is API consumption and the whole portfolio, and Studio is the vehicle even when it is not named</h2>',
  '<h2>2. BlackRock tells investors growth will come from API calls and the whole portfolio. Both run through Studio, though BlackRock rarely says the name</h2>')
R('<p>At its <a href="https://s24.q4cdn.com/856567660/files/doc_presentations/2025/BLK-Investor-Day-2025-WEB.pdf">2025 Investor Day</a>, on a slide about keeping the cost of growth low, BlackRock projected that API calls across BlackRock and Aladdin would grow 17x by 2030, against 4x for model portfolios, 2x for portfolios and 1.3x for trade allocations. The slide gave no starting number and does not say which products generate the API calls, so attributing them to Studio is my inference<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s1">1</a></sup>. API use is the activity the <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/platforms/products/aladdin-studio">Studio page</a> describes<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s13">13</a></sup>. Across <a href="https://www.roic.ai/quote/BLK/transcripts">26 earnings calls</a> from the first quarter of 2020 to the second quarter of 2026, no executive said “Studio”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s4">4</a></sup>; a single line on a <a href="https://s24.q4cdn.com/856567660/files/doc_presentations/2025/BlackRock-2023-Investor-Day.pdf">2023 Investor Day slide</a> is the only other investor mention found<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s53">53</a></sup>. BlackRock’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2012383/000119312526071966/R41.htm">10-K</a> says technology services fees are generally set by the value of positions on the platform or at a fixed rate, and can depend on subscriptions, go-lives and support; API calls appear in none of those terms<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s10">10</a>, <a href="#s1">1</a></sup>.</p>',
  '<p>BlackRock projected at its <a href="https://s24.q4cdn.com/856567660/files/doc_presentations/2025/BLK-Investor-Day-2025-WEB.pdf">2025 Investor Day</a> that API calls across BlackRock and Aladdin would grow 17x by 2030<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s1">1</a></sup>. That was the fastest-growing measure on the slide, which was about keeping the cost of growth low. Model portfolios were projected at 4x, portfolios at 2x and trade allocations at 1.3x. The slide gives no starting number and does not say which products generate the API calls. Attributing them to Studio is my inference<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s1">1</a></sup>. I make it because API use is the activity the <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/platforms/products/aladdin-studio">Studio page</a> describes<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s13">13</a></sup>. No executive said “Studio” on any of the <a href="https://www.roic.ai/quote/BLK/transcripts">26 earnings calls</a> from the first quarter of 2020 to the second quarter of 2026<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s4">4</a></sup>. Outside the calls, the only investor-facing mention I found is a single line on a <a href="https://s24.q4cdn.com/856567660/files/doc_presentations/2025/BlackRock-2023-Investor-Day.pdf">2023 Investor Day slide</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s53">53</a></sup>. BlackRock’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2012383/000119312526071966/R41.htm">10-K</a> says technology services fees are generally set by the value of positions on the platform or at a fixed rate. They can also depend on subscriptions, go-lives and support. API calls appear in none of those fee terms<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s10">10</a>, <a href="#s1">1</a></sup>. So the measure BlackRock is growing fastest is not one it says it charges for.</p>')
R('<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 12.</span> Mentions of Studio terms on 26 BlackRock earnings calls, Q1 2020 to Q2 2026</p>',
  '<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 12.</span> On 26 earnings calls from Q1 2020 to Q2 2026, no BlackRock executive said “Studio”, “citizen developer” or “agent”; Data Cloud, API and Copilot came up four times in total</p>')
R('<td>One line on a <a href="https://s24.q4cdn.com/856567660/files/doc_presentations/2025/BlackRock-2023-Investor-Day.pdf">2023 Investor Day slide</a> is the only other investor mention found<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s53">53</a></sup></td>',
  '<td>Never on a call. The only investor-facing mention found is one line on a <a href="https://s24.q4cdn.com/856567660/files/doc_presentations/2025/BlackRock-2023-Investor-Day.pdf">2023 Investor Day slide</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s53">53</a></sup></td>')
R('<a href="#s55">55</a></sup>, as an add-on sold with Aladdin Enterprise</td>',
  '<a href="#s55">55</a></sup>, both times described as an add-on sold with Aladdin Enterprise</td>')
R('Q1 2020 to Q2 2026<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s4">4</a></sup>. My tally. Zero counts are reliable; other counts are approximate.</p>',
  'Q1 2020 to Q2 2026<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s4">4</a></sup>. My tally. A zero means the term appears in no transcript; the non-zero counts are approximate.</p>')
R('<p>The other half of the growth story is the whole portfolio. BlackRock bought eFront in 2019 and Preqin in 2025, and in September 2025 its private markets team described the result on a <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/expertise/webinars/unifying-the-whole-portfolio-across-public-and-private-markets">client webinar</a>: investors “now have access to over 15,000 fund investments within eFront Insight” for benchmarking; Preqin indices “are also available today in Aladdin Explore and our Aladdin Data Cloud capabilities”; and “over 10 different transaction types” were added to Aladdin, across commitments, fundings, paydowns and PIK, so private credit loans are captured natively, designed with BlackRock’s own professionals and “our new colleagues from HPS”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s6">6</a></sup>. <a href="https://www.preqin.com/about/press-release/blackrock-aladdin-expands-private-credit-solutions-on-preqin-for-greater-transparency-and-insights-across-the-asset-class">Private credit analytics on Preqin</a> followed in May 2026<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s12">12</a></sup>. Newman lists eFront data and Preqin among what is joining Data Cloud<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s15">15</a></sup>. Hosting moved the same way: Microsoft wrote in December 2024 that <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-cloud/blog/2024/12/11/how-blackrock-is-finding-new-alpha-as-ai-transformation-becomes-a-critical-business-driver/">all BlackRock’s technology platform clients and services are hosted in the Azure cloud</a>, with new rollouts taking “weeks instead of quarters”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s58">58</a></sup>, and in December 2025 BlackRock and AWS announced a second option, with <a href="https://press.aboutamazon.com/aws/2025/12/blackrock-partners-with-aws-to-deliver-aladdin-on-secure-scalable-cloud-infrastructure">general availability for US-hosted Aladdin Enterprise clients expected in the second half of 2026</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s14">14</a></sup>.</p>',
  '<p>The other half of the growth story is the whole portfolio, public and private markets together. BlackRock bought eFront in 2019 and Preqin in 2025. In September 2025 its private markets team described the result on a <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/expertise/webinars/unifying-the-whole-portfolio-across-public-and-private-markets">client webinar</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s6">6</a></sup>. Investors “now have access to over 15,000 fund investments within eFront Insight” for benchmarking. Preqin indices “are also available today in Aladdin Explore and our Aladdin Data Cloud capabilities”. And “over 10 different transaction types” were added to Aladdin, covering commitments, fundings, paydowns and PIK, so private credit loans are captured natively. The team said those were designed with BlackRock’s own professionals and “our new colleagues from HPS”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s6">6</a></sup>. <a href="https://www.preqin.com/about/press-release/blackrock-aladdin-expands-private-credit-solutions-on-preqin-for-greater-transparency-and-insights-across-the-asset-class">Private credit analytics on Preqin</a> followed in May 2026<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s12">12</a></sup>. Newman, the Studio general manager, names eFront data and Preqin among the data being added to Data Cloud<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s15">15</a></sup>. Hosting has moved to the public cloud as well. Microsoft wrote in December 2024 that <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-cloud/blog/2024/12/11/how-blackrock-is-finding-new-alpha-as-ai-transformation-becomes-a-critical-business-driver/">all BlackRock’s technology platform clients and services are hosted in the Azure cloud</a>, with new rollouts taking “weeks instead of quarters”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s58">58</a></sup>. In December 2025 BlackRock and AWS announced a second option, with <a href="https://press.aboutamazon.com/aws/2025/12/blackrock-partners-with-aws-to-deliver-aladdin-on-secure-scalable-cloud-infrastructure">general availability for US-hosted Aladdin Enterprise clients expected in the second half of 2026</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s14">14</a></sup>.</p>')
R('Extensibility and data access are where BlackRock itself says growth is. The fastest-growing measure it showed investors is API calls, and the data it is adding, private markets and Preqin, arrives through Data Cloud and Explore, which are Studio’s surfaces. Studio is the vehicle for that story whether or not the name appears.</p>',
  'BlackRock itself says growth will come from extensibility and data access. The fastest-growing measure it showed investors is API calls. The data it is adding, private markets and Preqin, arrives through Data Cloud and Explore, and both of those are part of Studio. So Studio carries the growth story even when BlackRock does not say its name.</p>')

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pages 11-12, Finding 3
R('<h2>3. BlackRock has built an AI front door and keeps it inside the platform, while data providers market theirs outside</h2>',
  '<h2>3. BlackRock keeps its AI front door inside the platform; data providers market theirs outside</h2>')
R('Two of its engineers, Brennan Rosales, AI Engineering Lead, and Pedro Vicente Valdez, Principal AI Engineer, describe a plugin registry with <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“couple of thousands agents and tools,”</a> where the first way a team onboards is “to define a tool as kind of mapping one-to-one to an existing Aladdin API already out in production”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup>. Copilot’s tools are Studio’s APIs; Microsoft’s Adrian Crockett put it in one line on BlackRock’s podcast: <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/podcast/microsoft-part-2-podcast">“There’s no copilot without plug-ins”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s59">59</a></sup>. BlackRock’s AI product head says the team is making sure <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/podcast/reducing-time-to-insight-how-ai-is-reshaping-risk-management">“the data model itself is structured for agentic use cases”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s60">60</a></sup>.</p>',
  'Two of its engineers, Brennan Rosales, AI Engineering Lead, and Pedro Vicente Valdez, Principal AI Engineer, describe how it works. Copilot draws on a registry of plugins, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“couple of thousands agents and tools,”</a> built by Aladdin’s engineering teams. The first way a team adds a tool is “to define a tool as kind of mapping one-to-one to an existing Aladdin API already out in production”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup>. So Copilot’s tools are Studio’s APIs. Microsoft’s Adrian Crockett made the dependence plain on BlackRock’s podcast: <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/podcast/microsoft-part-2-podcast">“There’s no copilot without plug-ins”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s59">59</a></sup>. The data layer is being shaped the same way: BlackRock’s AI product head says the team is making sure <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/aladdin/discover/podcast/reducing-time-to-insight-how-ai-is-reshaping-risk-management">“the data model itself is structured for agentic use cases”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s60">60</a></sup>.</p>')
R('<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 13.</span> How Aladdin Copilot answers a question</p>',
  '<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 13.</span> Every Copilot answer is a call to a production Aladdin API: thousands of registered tools are filtered to 20 or 30, the call is planned and checked, and advice is refused</p>')
R('<div class="fbox" style="width:100%"><b>One question</b>, with the app, portfolio and widgets on screen; the talk’s example is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“what is my exposure to aerospace in Portfolio 1?”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></div>',
  '<div class="fbox" style="width:100%"><b>One question</b>, plus what is on screen: the open app, portfolio and widgets go in as context. The talk’s example is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“what is my exposure to aerospace in Portfolio 1?”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></div>')
R('<div class="fbox" style="width:75%"><b>20 to 30 candidates</b>: filtering by environment, user group and app leaves <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“say, 20 to 30 tools and agents,”</a> since more than “40, 50 tools” would hurt performance<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></div>',
  '<div class="fbox" style="width:75%"><b>20 to 30 tools in play</b>: Copilot filters the registry by environment, user group and app, leaving <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“say, 20 to 30 tools and agents,”</a> because giving the model more than “40, 50 tools” would hurt performance<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></div>')
R('<div class="fbox ai" style="width:62%"><b>Plan and call the API</b>: the orchestrator iterates <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“over a planning and action node”</a> with GPT-4 function calling, then output guardrails check for hallucinations<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></div>',
  '<div class="fbox ai" style="width:62%"><b>Plan and call the API</b>: an orchestrator, the component that decides which tool to call, loops <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“over a planning and action node”</a> using GPT-4’s function calling; a final check, the output guardrails, screens the answer for hallucinations<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></div>')
R('<div class="fbox core" style="width:49%"><b>One answer</b>, never investment advice: the system prompt is tested so Copilot will <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“never provide investment advice”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup>; the product page says the same<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s16">16</a></sup></div>',
  '<div class="fbox core" style="width:49%"><b>One answer</b>, never investment advice: Copilot’s standing instructions (its system prompt) are tested so it will <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oyqeCHFM5U4">“never provide investment advice”</a><sup class="fnref"><a href="#s9">9</a></sup>; the product page says the same<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s16">16</a></sup></div>')
R('The tool counts are the speakers’ own approximations; the talk is a machine transcription, marked in the register.</p>',
  'The tool counts are the speakers’ own approximations; the talk is a machine transcription, marked as such in the Sources section.</p>')
R('<p>Outside the login, the data market has chosen the other model. <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/">MCP</a> is the common standard AI assistants use to pull data from other systems<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s61">61</a></sup>. Seven providers that a Studio buyer also deals with offer a connector or an MCP server; LSEG had <a href="https://www.investing.com/news/company-news/lseg-q1-2026-slides-record-growth-ai-adoption-accelerates-93CH-4631412">90 customers connected</a> by April 2026, according to a secondary summary of its results slides<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s62">62</a>, <a href="#s63">63</a></sup>. No public connector for Aladdin was found on BlackRock’s pages or GitHub<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s13">13</a>, <a href="#s64">64</a></sup>; a <a href="https://careers.blackrock.com/job/mumbai/forward-deploy-engineer-aladdin-data-associate/45831/97466309856">Forward Deploy Engineer posting</a> for Aladdin Data names “Model Context Protocol (MCP), AI agents, and Copilot extensions”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s65">65</a></sup>.</p>',
  '<p>Outside the login, data providers have chosen the opposite design: they let the buyer’s own AI tools reach their data. <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/">MCP</a> is the common standard AI assistants use to pull data from other systems<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s61">61</a></sup>. Seven providers that Studio buyers also use offer a connector or an MCP server. LSEG had <a href="https://www.investing.com/news/company-news/lseg-q1-2026-slides-record-growth-ai-adoption-accelerates-93CH-4631412">90 customers connected</a> by April 2026, according to a secondary summary of its results slides<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s62">62</a>, <a href="#s63">63</a></sup>. I found no public connector for Aladdin on BlackRock’s pages or GitHub<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s13">13</a>, <a href="#s64">64</a></sup>. The one public hint is a job advert: a <a href="https://careers.blackrock.com/job/mumbai/forward-deploy-engineer-aladdin-data-associate/45831/97466309856">Forward Deploy Engineer posting</a> for Aladdin Data names “Model Context Protocol (MCP), AI agents, and Copilot extensions”<sup class="fnref"><a href="#s65">65</a></sup>. Exhibit 14 lists the data vendors, which are one comparison a Studio buyer makes, and the system-of-record rivals, which are the other.</p>')
R('<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 14.</span> Seven data and platform providers let outside AI tools pull their data; no public Aladdin equivalent was found</p>',
  '<span class="ex-num">Exhibit 14.</span> Six data and cloud providers have shipped AI connectors to their data and two system-of-record rivals have announced AI agents; Aladdin has no public equivalent, only a job advert that mentions MCP</p>')
